---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import ThoughtProcess from '../../components/ThoughtProcess.astro';
import MethodBars from '../../components/MethodBars.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const repo = 'https://github.com/omkar-k0/faultline';
const res = (f: string) => `${repo}/blob/main/eval/results/${f}`;

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '89.6%', label: 'root cause found at top-1 on dev (43 of 48 incidents)', evidence: 'measured', source: 'eval/results/dev/triage.md' },
  { value: '0 of 48', label: 'confidently wrong answers on dev; 0 of 30 false alarms on clean runs', evidence: 'measured', source: 'eval/results/dev/triage.md' },
  { value: '192', label: 'injected incidents across 8 fault types, plus 70 clean runs', evidence: 'measured', source: 'benchmark/manifest.json, release benchmark-v1' },
  { value: '—', label: 'held-out score: the frozen test split has not been run yet', evidence: 'pending', source: 'benchmark-heldout.tar.gz is sealed until the method is final' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Open source' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'November 2026 · in progress' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Designed by me, code written with Claude' },
  { label: 'Scale', value: '4 dbt projects · 193 tests · 5 methods scored' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Milestone 5 done, 6 in progress · benchmark v1 released' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'The gate' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'Thought process' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'refuses', label: 'What it refuses to claim' },
  { id: 'broke', label: 'What broke' },
  { id: 'open', label: 'Still open' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="faultline"
  question="When a company's data breaks, what broke it, and where is the evidence?"
  description="An open-source reliability assistant for dbt Core with its own fault-injection benchmark: 192 incidents, five methods scored, a sealed held-out split."
  crumb="Open source · 02"
  links={[
    { label: 'Source on GitHub', href: repo, primary: true },
    { label: 'Benchmark v1', href: `${repo}/releases/tag/benchmark-v1` },
    { label: 'Results on dev', href: `${repo}/tree/main/eval/results/dev` },
  ]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  tldr={{
    why: 'When company data breaks, reports and dashboards are wrong until someone finds the cause, and that can take hours of digging. This points straight at the cause and shows the evidence.',
    problem: 'When the automated steps that prepare a company\'s data break, an engineer digs through checks, logs and recent code changes for hours to find out why.',
    result: 'Found the exact cause in 43 of 48 test breakdowns (89.6%), never named a wrong cause and raised no false alarms. The final test, on breakdowns it has never seen, is still to come.',
    role: 'I researched and designed it and made the decisions, after talking options through with Claude for efficiency. Claude wrote the code; we debugged it together.',
  }}
  reproduce={{
    repo,
    commands: `git clone ${repo} && cd faultline
make setup            # uv env, install, build the 4 dbt test projects
make check            # ruff, mypy --strict, dbt build, pytest
make fetch-benchmark  # loads the train and dev incidents
make eval-dev         # scores every method on dev, writes eval/results/dev/`,
    files: [
      { path: 'eval/results/dev/triage.md', what: 'The 89.6% table, per fault type, with config hash' },
      { path: 'eval/results/dev/ablations.md', what: 'Triage without tests and without lineage' },
      { path: 'benchmark/manifest.json', what: 'The 192 incidents and 70 clean runs in release benchmark-v1' },
      { path: 'docs/EVAL.md', what: 'How top-1, top-3 and "not confirmed" are counted' },
    ],
    note: 'The held-out split ships sealed and stays unopened until the method is frozen.',
  }}
  stack={['Python 3.12', 'dbt Core', 'DuckDB', 'sqlglot', 'networkx', 'hypothesis', 'Ollama (qwen2.5 7B)', 'GitHub Actions']}
  prev={{ href: '/work/guidance-reconciliation-engine/', label: 'Guidance Reconciliation Engine' }}
  next={{ href: '/work/werkbank/', label: 'Werkbank' }}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      Data teams build their reporting tables with dbt. When a number on a dashboard is suddenly wrong, someone
      has to find out why: bad data arriving, a column renamed upstream, a join that started duplicating rows,
      or a code change last Tuesday.
    </p>
    <p>
      faultline does that search. It watches the tables, groups the symptoms into one incident, proposes
      possible causes, and tests each one against the recorded data. It only names a cause that passed its
      test. If none did, it says "not confirmed".
    </p>
    <p>
      To know whether that works, I first built the exam: a tool that breaks real dbt projects on purpose, in
      eight different ways, and records the right answer for every break.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      dbt's own tests catch some failures. How many root causes can a system name exactly, down to the table,
      column and commit, without ever pointing confidently at the wrong one?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>dbt tests</strong> are the standard safety net. On the dev split they raised an alert for 22.9% of
      incidents and named the cause for none. A test tells you that a table is wrong, not why.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pasting the logs into a language model</strong> is the popular shortcut. With a local 7B model, it
      named the right cause once in 48 incidents and a wrong cause seven times.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both share the same gap: neither checks a guess against the data before saying it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gate">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>The gate</h2>
    <p>
      Every decision goes through the same order: deterministic lineage and checks first, then statistics, and
      a language model last. The model's only planned job is to rank causes that have already passed a test
      and to write the report. No raw rows ever reach a model.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Job</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Lineage</td><td>Table and column lineage from compiled SQL; what feeds what</td><td>Built</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Monitors</td><td>Seven checks: schema, volume, freshness, null rate, distribution, duplicates, time shift</td><td>Built</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triage</td><td>Group symptoms, propose causes, test each one, keep only survivors</td><td>Built (deterministic)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local LLM</td><td>Re-rank survivors and write the report</td><td>Built, not the default (see below)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The headline number on this page comes from the deterministic layers alone. The local model re-ranker
      is built and was scored on the same 48 dev incidents. Off the shelf it did worse: 42 of 48 right and
      1 wrong, against 43 and 0 without it. Tuned on the train split it matched the rules (43 of 48, 0 wrong)
      but did not beat them. So the rules rank by default and the model stays optional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="system">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Thought process</h2>
    <p>Left to right, the path one incident takes. Each card hands the next one less uncertainty.</p>
    <ThoughtProcess
      steps={[
        { title: 'Gather', points: ['dbt artefacts and run logs', 'Monitor anomalies, failed tests', 'Lineage of this run, cached'], takeaway: 'Symptoms, not guesses.' },
        { title: 'Group', points: ['Symptoms joined by table lineage', 'One incident, not ten alerts', 'Stored in DuckDB'], takeaway: 'One problem, one report.' },
        { title: 'Propose', points: ['Data causes at seeds and sources', 'Code causes at changed models', 'With the commit that changed them'], takeaway: 'Every place it could have started.' },
        { title: 'Test', points: ['One check per cause', 'On aggregates, lineage, SQL, history', 'Failing causes are dropped'], takeaway: 'Guesses must survive the data.' },
        { title: 'Rank and report', points: ['Survivors only', '"Not confirmed" if none survive', 'LLM re-ranker: optional, no gain on dev'], takeaway: 'Never a confident wrong answer.', model: true },
      ]}
      note="The highlighted card is where the optional local model sits. It only ever sees causes that already passed a test, never the data itself."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>Decisions, and what they cost</h2>
    <div class="decisions">
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Build the exam before the student</strong>
          <p>A fault injector and 192 scored incidents came before any diagnosis code.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Demo on hand-picked examples</strong>
          <p>Hand-picked examples always work. A benchmark tells you how often.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>A sealed held-out project</strong>
          <p>The jira split (58 runs) was generated by me on my own machine and never opened in the coding session. A CI guard blocks any code that names it.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Tune and test on the same incidents</strong>
          <p>Whoever writes the rules must never see the final exam.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Score at location level</strong>
          <p>A cause counts only if the table, the column (when there is one) and, for code faults, the commit are right.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Score "right table"</strong>
          <p>The table-only score is reported, but only as a diagnostic, never as the headline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Code faults must change something</strong>
          <p>A code mutation is kept only if the build fails or a table profile changes.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Keep every mutation</strong>
          <p>A change with no symptom has no correct answer and would only add noise.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Lineage follows the SQL</strong>
          <p>When compiled SQL reads a table dbt did not declare, the edge is kept and reported.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Trust dbt's declared dependencies</strong>
          <p>The SQL is what runs. The Stripe project has two such hidden reads.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What came out</h2>
    <figure class="shot term">
      <p class="term-bar label">$ faultline eval --split dev --baseline triage</p>
      <pre><code>| Fault type          | n  | Top-1          | Wrong top-1 |
|---------------------|----|----------------|-------------|
| schema_change       | 6  | 100.0% (6/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| late_or_missing     | 6  | 100.0% (6/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| duplicate_keys      | 6  | 100.0% (6/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| null_spike          | 6  | 100.0% (6/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| distribution_shift  | 6  |  66.7% (4/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| model_logic_bug     | 6  |  83.3% (5/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| broken_join         | 6  | 100.0% (6/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| timezone_error      | 6  |  66.7% (4/6)   | 0.0% (0/6)  |
| all faulted         | 48 |  89.6% (43/48) | 0.0% (0/48) |

Without the tests (every proposed cause ranked) top-1 would be 39.6% (19/48).
False alarms on clean runs: 0.0% (0/30).</code></pre>
      <figcaption class="caption">Output of the tool, trimmed to three columns. Full table: <a href="https://github.com/omkar-k0/faultline/blob/main/eval/results/dev/triage.md" rel="noopener">eval/results/dev/triage.md</a> (config hash 2d6665853400).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Five methods on the same 48 dev incidents. Teal is the share of root causes named exactly. Grey is the
      share named confidently and wrongly.
    </p>
    <MethodBars
      rightLabel="right cause at top-1"
      wrongLabel="wrong cause at top-1"
      rows={[
        { name: 'dbt tests', note: 'alert only: fired on 11 of 48, names no cause', right: [0, 48], wrong: [0, 48] },
        { name: 'dbt tests + guess', note: 'alert plus naive cause', right: [0, 48], wrong: [11, 48] },
        { name: 'Raw logs → LLM', note: 'local qwen2.5 7B', right: [1, 48], wrong: [7, 48] },
        { name: 'faultline monitors', note: 'anomalies, ranked upstream-first', right: [27, 48], wrong: [16, 48] },
        { name: 'faultline triage', note: 'monitors + tested causes', right: [43, 48], wrong: [0, 48], ours: true },
      ]}
      caption="Dev split, 48 faulted incidents. Each method also had 0 of 30 false alarms on clean runs. Files: eval/results/dev/*.md."
    />
    <p>
      The monitors alone notice that something is wrong in 43 of 48 incidents (89.6%). But they name the right
      cause in only 27 (56.2%) and a wrong one in 16. Testing every proposed cause removes all of those wrong answers. The 5 incidents triage misses are
      ones the monitors never flagged: two distribution shifts, two timezone errors and one logic bug.
    </p>
    <p>
      The train split gave a similar picture: 86 of 96 right at top-1 (89.6%), 3 wrong, and 7 never flagged
      by the monitors at all. (This is the run after the train split was fixed on 19 Nov; the first run said
      85 of 96.)
      Both results reproduce byte for byte, and a test checks that they still do.
    </p>
    <p class="caption">Sources: <a href={res('dev/triage.md')}>triage</a>, <a href={res('dev/monitors.md')}>monitors</a>, <a href={res('dev/raw_log_llm.md')}>raw_log_llm</a>, <a href={res('dev/dbt_tests.md')}>dbt_tests</a>, <a href={res('dev/dbt_tests_guess.md')}>dbt_tests_guess</a>, <a href={res('train/triage.md')}>train/triage</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="refuses">
    <h2><span class="num">08</span>What it refuses to claim</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A held-out score.</strong> Dev is where the method was developed, so 89.6% is an optimistic
      number. The held-out jira split will be run once, when the method is final. That number goes here, good
      or bad.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>That abstaining works.</strong> "Not confirmed" never fired on dev or train. The behaviour exists
      in code but the benchmark has not tested it yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>That the model helps.</strong> It does not, yet. The base local model made one confident wrong
      call the rules did not make; the tuned one only tied them. Every headline number is from deterministic
      code. Source: <a href="https://github.com/omkar-k0/faultline/blob/main/eval/results/dev/llm_slots.md" rel="noopener">eval/results/dev/llm_slots.md</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="broke">
    <h2><span class="num">09</span>What broke</h2>
    <h3>A green build with empty tables</h3>
    <p>
      The first Stripe build passed 105 of 105 tests while every staging table was empty. dbt had read the
      sources before the seed data loaded. Now seeds load first, and a test fails if any model is empty.
    </p>
    <h3>The answers were in the logs</h3>
    <p>
      The saved git history still carried the injector's own commit messages, which described each fault. Any
      method could have read the answer. They are now removed at load time, and a leak test runs on every dev
      evaluation.
    </p>
    <h3>Realistic noise broke clean runs</h3>
    <p>
      Dropping a few percent of rows to simulate real data left orders without payments and failed tests on
      runs that were meant to be clean. Clean runs now vary only by 2% noise on numbers. The cost: volume
      false alarms became an easy test. That is listed as a known weakness.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2><span class="num">10</span>Still open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Milestone 6, in progress: the tuned local model and a hosted reference line.</li>
      <li>One run on the sealed held-out split.</li>
      <li>Incidents where "not confirmed" is the right answer, so abstaining gets tested.</li>
      <li>court_cases is a synthetic project I wrote. Real public court data comes after a licence check.</li>
      <li>Drafting fixes as pull requests (milestone 7) and a read-only MCP server (milestone 8).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
